    {
        "content": "<p>I think you need <code>Countable ι</code></p>",
        "id": 613614178,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1785407141
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>When <code>ι</code> is countable, <code>Finset ι</code> is also countable, and then from <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/MeasurableSpace/Defs.html#MeasurableSingletonClass.toDiscreteMeasurableSpace\">MeasurableSingletonClass.toDiscreteMeasurableSpace</a> and  <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/MeasurableSpace/Constructions.html#Finset.instMeasurableSingletonClass\">Finset.instMeasurableSingletonClass</a> you'll know that the measurable space structure on <code>Finset ι</code> is discrete.</p>",
        "id": 613615297,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1785407593
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For a counterexample in the case that <code>ι</code> is uncountable you can consider <code>ι = ℝ</code> , <code>M = ℕ</code>, and <code>f = 1</code> is the constant function. Then <code>∑ i ∈ sf.1, sf.2 i</code> is just the cardinality of the finset. If it is measurable, then<code>{s : Finset ι∣ s.card = 0} = {∅} = ∩ i, ​{ s ∣ i ∈ s }</code> is measurable as this is an uncountable intersection (you can be more rigorous by using <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Constructions/Cylinders.html#MeasureTheory.MeasurableSet.eq_preimage_restrict_countable\">MeasurableSet.eq_preimage_restrict_countable</a>)</p>",
        "id": 613616373,
        "sender_full_name": "Yongxi Lin (Aaron)",
        "timestamp": 1785407999
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess you're right... thank you</p>",
